<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">69 CE</h1><p class="ref-lede">69 CE in world history: 5 events and 2 deaths. 139 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/31-bce">31 BCE</a><a href="/years/30-bce">30 BCE</a><a href="/years/27-bce">27 BCE</a><a href="/years/23">23 CE</a><a href="/years/69" aria-current="page">69 CE</a><a href="/years/224">224 CE</a><a href="/years/260">260 CE</a><a href="/years/328">328 CE</a><a href="/years/357">357 CE</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-15">15 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Emperor Galba murdered in the Roman Forum</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Praetorians loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otho">Otho</a> killed the elderly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galba">Galba</a> after just seven months as emperor. Otho, then Vitellius and finally Vespasian took power in the turmoil known as the Year of the Four Emperors.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-14">14 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Vitellius's army defeats Otho at Bedriacum</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Troops loyal to Vitellius beat Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otho">Otho</a>'s forces near Cremona in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Otho took his own life two days later, but Vitellius himself fell before the year was out.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-21">21 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Senate recognises Vespasian as emperor</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The day after Vitellius was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the Senate granted Vespasian the powers of emperor, ending the Year of the Four Emperors. His Flavian dynasty restored stability and began the Colosseum.</p></div></li></ol></section>
